Chapter 17 – Part 5
Curtis fell asleep first.
It happened without announcement — the tension in his shoulders loosening by degrees, breath lengthening, the last thin line of watchfulness finally giving way. Long black braids spilled across the pillow. His arm remained loosely around her even in sleep, as if the body had accepted the arrangement before the mind finished arguing about it.
Celty stayed awake.
Black smoke rose in slow, quiet ribbons from the empty space above her collar as the room settled into the deeper hush of a shared night. With no more conversation to manage, her thoughts drifted whether she wanted them to or not.
And they drifted, inevitably, toward comparison.
Shinra was familiar. Narrower through the shoulders. Lighter in the arms. A body shaped more by indoor years, precise hands, and the sedentary gravity of a man who lived among instruments and theories. She knew that body. Had known it for years in fragments and careful distances. It had never been about bulk with him. It had been about continuity. Presence. The strange devotion of someone who had grown up beside her and never fully stopped staring.
Curtis was different under her hand.
Broader. Heavier in the chest and shoulders. Muscle laid down by necessity rather than vanity, the kind that remained even at rest because the life under it had required force too many times. Even asleep, there was a density to him that made the mattress feel more occupied. The compression shirt did little to hide it; if anything, it only mapped the shape more honestly. Where Shinra felt like someone she could haul upright out of trouble, Curtis felt like someone the trouble would have to reconsider.
The comparison made her uneasy the moment it finished forming.
Not because it was shallow — though there was a physical truth in it she could not pretend not to notice — but because of what it implied. She was lying in another man’s bed, measuring the body against the one she had called home for years, and the measurement was not only about size. It was about the way strength sat on them. Shinra’s care often came with fascination and ownership. Curtis’s strength came with scars, fatigue, and a kind of uncalculated vulnerability that had ended with him flushed on the floor and then asleep with an arm around her.
Black smoke curled tighter above her collar.
This is dangerous, she thought.
Not the muscles.
The fact that her mind would compare them so easily now, and that one of the comparisons kept ending in Curtis’s favor for reasons that had nothing to do with the shape of a shoulder.
Celty shifted slightly under the covers, only meaning to settle her hand more comfortably against the line of his side.
Instead, her gloved fingers brushed against something solid, warm, and unmistakably larger than she had accounted for.
She froze.
If she had eyes, they would have widened.
What the—
The thought arrived raw and unfiltered. Curtis was bigger in other ways as well. Not in the abstract, not as rumor or implication — as a present fact under the blanket, impossible to misread once noticed. The comparison her mind had already been making to Shinra took on one more inconvenient dimension and then promptly became worse for it.
Black smoke spiked once above her collar before she forced it back under control.
She did not snatch her hand away so hard it would wake him. She also did not leave it where it was. The withdrawal was careful, almost embarrassed in its precision, as if discretion could undo the information her sense of touch had already filed permanently.
Of course, she thought, equal parts dry and flustered. Of course the man built like a final obstacle would be proportional about it.
The private channel stayed sealed. No voice. No comment for the sleeping figure beside her. Only the internal static of a headless woman who had come to the room for honesty and emotional proximity, and had instead received one additional, deeply impractical data point about the foreigner currently asleep with an arm around her.
She stared at the ceiling she could not properly see and, for the first time all night, was grateful he was unconscious.
Celty should have left the discovery alone.
She did not.
Against better judgment, against every practical instinct that had kept her alive and relatively sane in a human city, she let her awareness return for a second look — more deliberate this time, less accidental. The covers, the angle, the undeniable present fact of him asleep on his back with no interest in performing or concealing anything.
The private reaction left her with no elegance at all.
Holy shit.
Black smoke burst upward from the empty space above her collar in a sharp, uncontrolled ribbon before she wrestled it down again. If the helmet had been on, she might have had the comfort of an opaque shell to hide behind. Without it, there was only the bare fact of her sitting in another man’s bed, newly in possession of information she had not asked for and could not now un-know.
Proportional had been an understatement.
The comparison to Shinra, already dangerous earlier in the night, became actively unhelpful. She cut it off mid-formation, only for it to return a second later like a bad signal. Curtis was not only broader in the shoulders and heavier through the chest. He was larger where it most inconveniently mattered, and the fact that he could be this unguarded in sleep while remaining completely unaware of her crisis only made the whole situation worse.
Celty turned her face-line toward the ceiling and remained very, very still.
I came here for emotional honesty, she told herself flatly.
Not a full inventory.
The black smoke settled into a thinner, mortified curl. Beside her, Curtis slept on, long black braids spilled across the pillow, one arm still loosely angled toward her as if the most shocking thing in the room were not currently happening entirely inside her own head.
Celty remained rigid for another few seconds, black smoke thinning above her collar as she forced the second look, the comparison, and the entire unhelpful inventory back into a box she could deal with never again.
…Maybe one day—
The thought formed before she could stop it.
No. That’s ridiculous.
She shut it down hard. Whatever room this night had opened, it was not an invitation to start drafting futures against the body of a man who had only just stopped bracing for loss long enough to fall asleep. Emotional honesty was one thing. Letting a single shared bed rewrite the architecture of her life before morning was another.
Still, exhaustion won where discipline only argued.
Her body settled by degrees. The earlier tension loosened. Black smoke above her collar slowed into the softer pattern of approaching rest. Without fully deciding to, she shifted closer under the covers, one pale leg sliding over his in an ordinary search for warmth and anchorage — and, in the process, came to rest over the same unmistakable fact that had already ruined her composure once.
This time she was too far gone to launch into another internal crisis about it.
Curtis slept on, long black braids spilled across the pillow, breath steady. Celty’s gloved hand remained loosely near his side as her awareness finally dimmed, the last clear thought of the night a half-formed, mortified note that the most dangerous part of staying had not been the conversation.
It had been how easily her body decided he was safe enough to sleep on.
The flat went quiet.
The city continued beyond the windows.
And for the first time since either of them had entered the room, both of them slept.
Elsewhere, Shinra’s apartment held none of the careful quiet of Curtis’s bedroom.
Mei was bent over his desk, pink hair spilled across scattered papers and the edge of a closed laptop, one of his hands braced at her hip while the other kept her pinned forward. There was no poetry in it and no pretense of complication — just the hard, simple rhythm of his cock buried in her ass, the desk complaining softly under every thrust. She pushed back to meet him with a breathless laugh that broke into a moan, completely uninterested in making the moment mean more than what it was.
“Harder,” she got out, voice rough with heat. “Stop thinking.”
Shinra did as told. Glasses slightly askew, shirt still half on, he drove into her with the focused intensity of a man who had spent too many years translating desire into theory and was, for once, being allowed to skip the translation. The wet sound of skin, the hitch in Mei’s breathing, the creak of the desk — all of it filled the room in place of confessions, scars, or careful emotional arithmetic.
With Mei, there was no lag between wanting and having.
No nonhuman distance to navigate.
No need to ask whether closeness would become a future grief.
Just her body under his hands, open and demanding, and the blunt relief of being able to take what was offered without turning it into a thesis.
Neither of them mentioned Celty.
Neither of them needed to.
The night was already split cleanly in two: one man asleep with a headless woman carefully choosing not to leave, and another buried to the hilt in the simpler version of intimacy he still pretended was secondary.